---
export const prerender = true;

import RoomLayout from '../../../layouts/RoomLayout.astro';
import ZybachNav from '../../../components/zybach/ZybachNav.astro';
import WorkingEditionBanner from '../../../components/zybach/WorkingEditionBanner.astro';

/* Audience: a first-time reader, family researcher or teacher choosing a
   useful source. Offer a concrete first reading in plain language, without
   assuming knowledge of the collection's catalog or project history.
   Every link on this page points at a page that exists in
   src/pages/collections/zybach/ (or one of its dynamic routes). Nothing here
   is a promise about a page that has not been built. */
---

<RoomLayout
  room="collections"
  title="Start here"
  subtitle="Choose a question, read the sources, and keep the evidence with your notes."
  width="prose"
  sourceId="collections-zybach-start-here"
  parents={[{ label: 'The Zybach Collection', href: '/collections/zybach/' }]}
>
  <span data-pagefind-meta="collection:zybach" hidden></span>

  <div class="source-page">
    <WorkingEditionBanner />
    <ZybachNav current="start-here" />

    <p>
      Bob Zybach’s collection connects people and Oregon landscapes through
      oral histories, photographs, maps, court papers and journals. Begin with
      one question in any of the four published series. The links below take
      you to short guided readings, with the fuller sources close at hand.
    </p>

    <section class="zc-first-reading" aria-labelledby="first-question">
      <h2 id="first-question">A first question to follow</h2>
      <ul class="zc-guide">
        <li>
          <a href="/collections/zybach/trails/a-valley-changes/">What changed
          in Soap Creek Valley?</a> — compare residents’ memories with the
          historic Auto Tour and an account of managing the research forest.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/collections/zybach/trails/letitia-in-court/">What does
          Letitia Carson’s court record show?</a> — follow a claim, an answer
          and two jury decisions through the transcribed documents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/collections/zybach/trails/dean-mountain/">What can one
          Elliott forest stop reveal?</a> — connect a field-trip video, an
          earlier interview and photographs of Dean Mountain.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/collections/zybach/trails/reading-1828/">How do we compare
          accounts of the 1828 Umpqua events?</a> — read a journal, a company
          report and a chief’s account recorded by a company officer.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each trail has four stops. Use it on your own, with a class or with a
        family member; bring back one observation and the source that supports it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="zc-first-reading" aria-labelledby="alberta-question">
      <h2 id="alberta-question">How can students study a changing neighborhood?</h2>
      <p>The Alberta Street project began with research by Portland high-school
        students. Its 1992 and 2015 reports offer two views of the same
        neighborhood, twenty-three years apart.</p>
      <p><a href="/collections/zybach/alberta-street/">Explore the Alberta Street
        source guide →</a> Follow reading questions into the original reports on
        ORWW and compare how the teams studied the neighborhood. This is a guide
        to those published sources; the reports and interviews are not reproduced
        in the Library.</p>
    </section>

    <h2 id="neighbor">If you live nearby, or you are simply curious</h2>

    <p>
      Read one life, straight through, the way you would read a short book.
      <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/m01-grabe/">Lorna Grabe</a> was the
      first one printed: a former schoolteacher on her family, and on the group
      that looks after the Soap Creek schoolhouse.
      <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/m05-rohner/">Jake and Willie
      Rohner</a> is a Swiss family's farming life on the north slope of Coffin
      Butte, from a farm bought at the end of one world war to its sale to the
      U.S. Army at the start of the next.
    </p>

    <p>
      Explore the <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/auto-tour/">1989
      Historic Soap Creek Valley Auto Tour</a> online, stop by stop. Each stop
      connects the original illustrated pages with the Library’s place records.
    </p>

    <h2 id="family">If you are looking for a family</h2>

    <ul class="zc-guide">
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/index/">Series Index</a> — start
        with a surname. Search the headings from all fifteen books, then follow
        their links to the printed pages. Try
        <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/index/?q=Moore">Moore</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/people/">People</a> — profiles of
        narrators, interviewers and contributors. Other people named in the
        books are found through the Series Index.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/places/">Places</a> — farms and
        landmarks, connected to the accounts that mention them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/letitia-carson/people/">People in the
        Letitia Carson records</a> — historical family members and others named
        in the documents, with links to the evidence. The collection does not
        publish living descendants’ personal details.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Every printed page here has its own address. Click the small page number
      in the margin and your browser's address bar will point at that one
      printed page — so you can send a relative the page, not the book, and
      they will land on the paragraph you meant.
      <a href="/collections/zybach/how-to-cite/#page-anchors">How to cite</a>
      shows what that looks like.
    </p>

    <h2 id="researcher">If you are researching or teaching</h2>

    <p>
      Use a guided trail as a source-comparison exercise. At each stop, record
      who is speaking or writing, when the record was made, and what it supports.
      Then compare two sources: where do they agree, where do they differ, and
      what would you need to check next? Keep a quotation separate from your
      own interpretation.
    </p>

    <ul class="zc-guide">
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/timeline/">Soap Creek timeline</a> — dated
        statements in the narrators' own words, arranged by period.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/soap-creek/thesis/#methodology">Bob’s
        research method in the 1999 thesis</a> — how interviews, maps,
        photographs and historical records were brought together to study
        changes in the valley.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/letitia-carson/documents/">Letitia Carson
        documents</a> — court, estate and homestead records. Distinguish a
        person’s claim from an answer or a decision, and follow the
        transcriptions back to the scans.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/jedediah-smith/the-1828-record/">The 1828
        record</a> — compare accounts of the Umpqua events and keep track of
        who preserved whose words. The chief’s account comes through a
        company officer’s written record.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/how-to-cite/">How to cite</a> — cite the
        printed book and the printed page; add the Library's address if you
        want a reader to be able to click.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/how-to-cite/#rights">Reuse and rights</a>,
        and <a href="/collections/zybach/provenance/">provenance</a> — who made
        each part, who published it, and what the Library did to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/collections/zybach/elliott/">The Elliott interviews and
        field trips</a> — follow a place through recordings, photographs and
        video, checking the date of each visit.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Finding the recordings.</strong> The Library has preserved copies
      of the Soap Creek and Elliott recordings supplied by Oregon State
      University. Those copies remain private during rights review. For archive
      access, use Oregon State’s
      <a href="https://scarc.library.oregonstate.edu/findingaids/?p=collections/findingaid&amp;id=2309">Soap
      Creek finding aid (OH 006)</a> or
      <a href="https://scarc.library.oregonstate.edu/findingaids/?p=collections/findingaid&amp;id=3293">Elliott
      finding aid (OH 047)</a>. Where a cassette has not survived, start with the
      printed transcript.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Where the originals are.</strong> Bob Zybach publishes all fifteen
      monographs, the thesis and the Auto Tour as PDFs on his own site, and the
      Elliott material on ORWW. The Library's pages point at his copies rather
      than replacing them; the links are on the
      <a href="/collections/zybach/provenance/">provenance page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="before">Two things to know before you read</h2>

    <p>
      Some of this is hard to read. People recalling the 1920s and 1930s use
      words that were ordinary then and are not now. Nothing has been softened
      or quietly edited out; the
      <a href="/collections/zybach/content-note/">content note</a> explains how
      the Library handles it and why.
    </p>

    <p>
      A few pages are held back. Where that happens the page says so, and says
      the reason, rather than leaving a gap you would have to guess at.
    </p>

    <p class="see-also">
      See also: <a href="/collections/zybach/">Finding aid</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/about-the-creator/">About the creator</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/colophon/">How this was made</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</RoomLayout>

<style>
  .zc-first-reading {
    margin: 1.75rem 0 2rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.35rem;
    background: var(--linen-card, #fffdf7);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-border, #d8d1c2);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--old-gold, #a87f2c);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .zc-first-reading h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .zc-first-reading p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .zc-first-reading a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--deep-navy, #14243c);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .zc-guide {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.4rem;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .zc-guide li {
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
